<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diadochi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diadochi ('successors') were the generals and companions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who fought over his empire after his death in 323 BCE. Their wars broke up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> and produced the Hellenistic kingdoms, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, founded by Ptolemy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diadochi.jpg" alt="Diadochi" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diadochi_LA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Argead line ended in the wars of the <b>Diadochi</b>: Philip III Arrhidaeus was executed by Olympias in 317 BCE and the boy Alexander IV put to death by Cassander in 311/310 BCE, and the Antipatrid and Antigonid dynasties held the throne until Rome abolished the monarchy in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His generals, the <b>Diadochi</b>, divided the empire through a series of wars (322–281 BCE), from which emerged the Ptolemaic dynasty in Egypt, the Seleucid Empire in Asia and the Antigonid kingdom in Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucid Empire emerged from the fragmentation of Alexander the Great's conquests after his death in Babylon in June 323 BCE, when his generals, the <b>Diadochi</b>, began competing for control of his vast inheritance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Diadochi</b> the city kept a relative autonomy, with kings such as Abdalonymus, and under Rome it prospered, embellished by Herod with a theatre, sheltering enough Jews in 66 CE that local pagans hesitated to attack them, and raised to colonial rank under Elagabalus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
